"""
Fig. 9 (revised) -- p_atm uncertainty, adjustments, and attribution.

Layout (see smartosse-manuscript/reviews/fig_revision_plan_reviewer2.md;
row 3 is a 2026-07-12 experiment, not yet in that plan doc):

    ROW 1 (2 panels): the two uncertainty priors
        (a) sigma_patm STD    (sub-daily std, the main-run prior)   [map + cable]
        (b) sigma_patm SPREAD (Chaudhuri reanalysis spread)          [map + cable]

    ROW 2 (4 panels): one per partial cable (LS, SPG, NS, Nfl)
        grey band       = +/- sigma_patm (SPREAD, at that cable's sensors), CONSTANT
        black, densely
        dotted          = Ponte & Dorandeau (2003) ~2 hPa RMS anchor, CONSTANT
        solid line      = daily |dp_atm| adjustment avg over sensors, STD run
        dashed line     = daily |dp_atm| adjustment avg over sensors, SPREAD run

    ROW 3 (4 panels, under row 2, same 4 columns): per-cable relcon, sideways
        two horizontal stacked bars per panel (STD on top, SPREAD below,
        winds/patm/other, each summing to 1) -- distinguished by the
        'std'/'spread' y-tick labels (optionally also hatch SPREAD via
        plot_relcon_bars_region's spread_hatch, off by default).
        Replaces the old single "(g)" 2-bar-mean-over-region panel (now
        unused but still defined, see plot_relcon_bars/load_relcon_std_spread)
        -- removing it let row 1 go back to a plain 2-panel a/b split.

Everything here is a small function taking already-loaded data (or a run
directory) and returning ``ax`` (or ``fig, ax``) so panels can be built,
tweaked, and re-laid-out interactively from a notebook. Spatial panels follow
the smartosse.plot conventions (``spna``); panels (a)/(b) plot lat/lon fields
directly (contourf + PlateCarree) rather than via ``ds.plotpc``.

NOTE (panel a/b source data): the ASTE-gridded `wApressure_*.bin` weight
files (`w = sigma^-2`, regridded onto the ASTE grid via MITgcm's EXF) are
what actually get fed into the OSSEs as control-uncertainty priors, but they
don't make good plots. Panels (a)/(b) instead use
`sigma_patm_{std,spread}_2012.nc` under `figures/data/`, generated by
`gen_patm_uncertainty_fields.py` directly from the JRA55/JRA3Q/ERA5
reanalysis products (see that script's docstring for the recipe, and for a
real lon-alignment bug it found and fixed along the way). Regenerate those
.nc files with:
    conda activate /work/08381/goldberg/ls6/miniforge3/envs/esmpy_3.10
    python -m smartosse.figures.gen_patm_uncertainty_fields
load_sigma_patm_std/_spread() (ASTE grid, from the weight binaries) are still
used for row 2's grey band, which needs to index sigma at cable (tile, j, i)
sensor locations.

NOTE (data availability): the FM run directories below live on
/scratch/08381/goldberg/aste_270x450x180/osses/. Some iter0000/ dirs and
xx_apressure.effective / data.ecco / data.ctrl files were missing on this
machine as of 2026-07-12 (likely not yet copied over) -- see the loader
docstrings for what each function needs.

NOTE (P&D 2003 anchor): we don't have the actual Ponte & Dorandeau (2003)
data; per Matt's estimate the SPNA value is roughly 4 hPa^2 (a mean-square
DIFFERENCE between reanalysis products, hence squared units), so the RMS
anchor plotted on the [hPa] row-2 axes is sqrt(4) = 2 hPa. Treat PD_2003_RMS_HPA
as a rough placeholder, not a citation-grade number.

NOTE (xx_apressure time ordering): load_patm_adjustment reproduces a time
reversal applied in patm.ipynb (``.sel(time=slice(None, None, -1))``) before
stamping calendar dates onto the control knots. That was empirically
necessary there but not independently re-derived here -- if a returned series
looks calendar-inverted, try ``reverse_time=False``. For a first-principles
control<->diagnostic time alignment (recommended for anything quantitative,
e.g. slope fits), see eta_ib_histogram.py's ctrl_t/diag_t interpolation
instead.
"""
